---



<Layout
  title="Udit Mahajan — Product Designer & Developer | Product Design, UX & Visual Systems"
  description="My journey into design, how I think about products, and what I value while working with teams and founders. A curated selection of product design, UX, and visual work focused on clarity, emotion, and real-world impact."
  pubDate={new Date()}
  >

  <CaseStudyHero
  title="Yelona"
  subtitle="Designing an AI-powered health marketplace that simplifies decision-making and builds lasting habits through personalized experiences."
  image="/Images/Sample.png"
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyIntro
  title="The Context"
  description={`While designing solutions at Krityam, I was brought in to architect an internal Inventory & Order Management System (IOMS) for Yelona. 

  Yelona is an online retail brand that sells a huge variety of products—ranging from apparel & footwear to fine jewelry, home goods, & electronics. They manage their own inventory & sell directly to customers across major Indian commerce channels like Amazon, IndiaMart & others.

I replaced a highly vulnerable manual spreadsheet setup with a unified digital system that protects real-time stock numbers, handles multi-component product sets, and stops manual data-entry errors before they hit live sales channels.`}
  role={["Product Designer", "UX Researcher"]}
  duration="Jan 2025 — Feb 2025"
  team={["1 Product Manager", "2 Developers", "1 Designer (Me)", "1 QA Tester"]}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyImpact
  decisionHeading="The Impact"
  decisionDescription="Before diving into how I mapped out the system architecture, let's look at the direct impact these design updates had on Yelona’s day-to-day operations. After the initial rollout of the IOMS, we tracked performance across our primary operational bottlenecks. Key Performance Indicators revealed:"

  impacts={[
    {
      label: "Bulk Processing & Data Entry Speed Improved by",
      value: "27%",
      trend: "up",
    },
    {
      label: "Reduction in Stock-In & Stock-Out Update Errors by",
      value: "48%",
      trend: "down",
    },
    {
      label: "Elimination of Composite Bundle Blindspots",
      value: "100%",
      trend: "up",
    },
  ]}
  detail={`Please note that certain custom algorithmic calculations and internal database structures remain protected under a strict non-disclosure agreement (NDA).`}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyPrologue
  eyebrow="OVERVIEW"
  title="About Yelona’s Business"
  description={`Yelona is an online retail brand that sells a huge variety of products—ranging from apparel & footwear to fine jewelry, home goods, & electronics. They manage, stock, and fulfill their own diverse product portfolio and sell across major e-commerce platforms  in India, including Amazon, Flipkart, Indiamart & other digital storefronts.`}
  image="/Images/cosmograph.png"
  note="Courtesy: Yelona"
  client:load
/>

<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 sm:px-10 pb-20">

<p class="text-base tracking-[0.2em] font-google font-bold text-zinc-500 uppercase">
  Research & Discovery
</p>

<h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-5xl font-google font-semibold text-zinc-50 leading-tight tracking-wide">
  Current Flow & Problems
</h2>

<p class="text-zinc-400 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">
  At the first glance, the founder's observation seemed spot-on: Eating healthy in India is an exhausting chore. Between hunting for healthy products, tracking complex calories, and aligning with personal health goals, the mental load on the average Indian consumer had reached a breaking point. So, to validate this hypothesis, I along with my manager, stepped out into the aisles of modern retail stores in Mumbai & discovered these pain points:
</p>

</section>

<CaseStudyPain
  title="Pain Points"
  description="Interviews and stakeholder workshops revealed recurring friction across admin teams, customers, and operations managers."
  challenges={[
    {
      title: "Admin & Multi-Tenant Pain Points",
      notes: [
        "/Ideas/idea1.png",
        "/Ideas/idea2.png",
        "/Ideas/idea3.png",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "Customer Pain Points",
      notes: [
        "/Ideas/idea4.png",
        "/Ideas/idea5.png",
        "/Ideas/idea6.png",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "Operations & Finance Pain Points",
      notes: [
        "/Ideas/idea7.png",
        "/Ideas/idea8.png",
        "/Ideas/idea8.png",
      ],
    },
  ]}
  client:load
/>

<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 sm:px-10 pt-25 pb-50">
  <div class="border-l-3 border-zinc-700 pl-6 md:pl-8">
  <p class="text-2xl tracking-wider font-google font-bold text-zinc-50">
    The Core Objective:
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-zinc-400 text-base md:text-xl leading-relaxed ">
    How might we transform slow, repetitive manual data-entry into a unified, automated, & highly responsive inventory engine for warehouse operators—reducing processing times and sync friction down to a few clicks?
  </p>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 sm:px-10 py-20">

<p class="text-base tracking-[0.2em] font-google font-bold text-zinc-500 uppercase">
  THE IDEATION
</p>

<h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-5xl font-google font-semibold text-zinc-50 leading-tight tracking-wide">
  Organising the Chaos
</h2>

<p class="text-zinc-400 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">
  To move beyond the limitations of manual Excel spreadsheets, I had to treat the entire business operation as a relational data model. I mapped out every moving part and defined the logic governing how they interact, ensuring that the system could automate inventory states without human intervention.
</p>

</section>

<CaseStudyGlossary
  decisionHeading="The Core System Elements"
  decisionDescription="I defined ten foundational components that make up the Yelona operational landscape:"
  factors={factors}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyPrologue
  eyebrow="INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE (IA)"
  title="Deconstructing the Architecture"
  description={`To prevent data mismatch and errors, I designed the Information Architecture around specific relational dependencies. If one element changes, it creates a calculated ripple effect across the entire system by establishing strict system rules and interconnections to ensure the software would automate background tasks flawlessly.`}
  image="/Images/cosmograph.png"
  note="Representational Graph"
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyElements
  decisionHeading="Mapping the Interconnections"
  decisionDescription="To prevent data drift and operational errors, I built the Information Architecture on a strict set of relational rules:"
  factors={[
    {
      source: "Vendor",
      target: "Individual Item",
      description:
        "Every individual item is mapped to exactly one vendor. An item doesn't have two or more different suppliers in Yelona's database.",
    },
    {
      source: "Composite Set",
      target: "Individual Items",
      description:
        "Composite Sets derive availability from their underlying Individual Items, specifically with the lowest-available required component.",
    },
    {
      source: "Warehouse",
      target: "Inventory",
      description:
        "Stock quantities are maintained per warehouse, providing precise visibility into inventory distribution.",
    },
    {
      source: "SKU Code",
      target: "Alias SKU Codes",
      description:
        "Each internal SKU Code can map to multiple Alias SKU Codes, but an Alias SKU can only point back to one internal SKU.",
    },
    {
      source: "Low Stock",
      target: "Purchase Order",
      description:
        "Low-Stock alerts are calculated per SKU and act as the trigger to initiate the Purchase Order (PO) draft lifecycle.",
    },
    {
      source: "Purchase Order",
      target: "Stock-In",
      description:
        "A Purchase Order (PO) is tied to one Vendor. A Stock-In entry is created against the PO, once its received at the warehouse.",
    }, 
  ]}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyProblem
  eyebrow="THE STRATEGY"
  title="The Cognitive Framework"
  description={`Designing for enterprise operations isn’t about making a system "look pretty"—it’s about reducing the psychological friction of repetitive, high-stakes tasks. Warehouse environments are loud, fast-paced, and can be mentally exhausting. If a back-office worker feels cognitive fatigue, they make data-entry mistakes that directly hurt the company's bottom line.

To build an interface that feels like an extension of the user's intent, I anchored my design decisions in the psychological realities of human error, visual fatigue, and spatial memory. I focused entirely on reducing the mental friction of managing massive data sets.`}
 problems={problems}
  client:load
/>

